course: dictionary
lesson: 12507
title: Depose: when a ruler loses power
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Depose is a strong verb. It means to remove a ruler or leader from power, often by force. Think of a king losing his crown and his throne. Kings and queens in history were deposed when their people rose up against them.
explain: «The people rose up and deposed the cruel king.» The clues are rose up and cruel: the people were angry, and the king lost his power. Ask what would fit in the sentence. Removed from power fits, so that is what deposed means here.
explain: Depose has a second meaning in law. To depose someone is to take their sworn statement, usually in writing, outside a court. «The witness was asked to depose before the trial.» Here it means giving a statement, not losing a crown, so the context tells you which meaning to use.
fact: Depose means to remove a ruler or leader from power, often by force.
fact: «The deposed king» means the king who has been removed from power.
fact: In law, to depose someone means to take their sworn statement, often outside a court.
fact: Depose is a verb: depose, deposes, deposed, deposing.
fact: In a history lesson, you might read that a king was deposed by a revolution.
exercise: «The army deposed the president in a night of fighting.» What does deposed mean here? | Elected to a new job | *Removed from power | Invited to a big party | Given a medal for bravery
exercise: «The deposed leader now lives far away from his old country.» What does deposed leader mean? | A leader who is very tall | *A leader who has been removed from power | A leader who has just been chosen by a vote | A leader who loves to sing
exercise: Lily says depose means to lose a game. Is she right? | Yes, it means to lose a game | No, it means to sing loudly | Yes, it means to win a prize | *No, it means to remove a ruler or leader from power
test: «Kings were deposed when people no longer trusted them.» What does deposed mean? | Sent on holiday | Crowned again | Praised in a song | *Removed from power
test: In law, what does depose mean? | Cook a meal for the judge | Put money in a bank | Paint the walls of a courtroom | *Give a sworn statement outside court
exam: Which sentence uses depose correctly to mean removed from power? | The teacher deposed a pile of books on the desk. | The baker deposed a loaf of bread into the oven. | *The crowd deposed the tyrant, and a new government took over. | The children deposed their toys in the box and ran outside.
